<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vadodara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vadodara, formerly Baroda, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Vishwamitri river. From the 1720s it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> Gaekwad dynasty, whose Baroda State became one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a> built the Laxmi Vilas Palace and promoted education, and the city's university bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vadodara.jpg" alt="Vadodara" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sharadsy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LakshmiVilas_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vadodara lies on the fertile plain between the Mahi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> rivers in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, at an elevation of 39 m. The Vishwamitri river, which frequently dries up in summer, runs through it. The city is the administrative headquarters of Vadodara district. Its name is said to come from the abundance of banyan (vad) trees, and by tradition it is a corrupt form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> vatodara, meaning &quot;in the belly of the banyan tree&quot;.</p><p>The city was once called Chandanavati after Raja Chandan of the Dod Parmar Rajputs, and its capital was known as Virakshetra, the Land of Warriors. Early English travellers and merchants of the 15th century call the town Baroda, and the official name was changed to Vadodara in 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At Akota, now a suburb, excavations have found microlith deposits showing habitation no later than 1000 BCE. By the first centuries CE Akota, known in antiquity as Aṅkoṭṭaka, was a commercial town with well-built brick houses and far-reaching trade links. It flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitraka-dynasty">Maitraka dynasty</a>, and a large hoard of Jain bronzes, including images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankaras</a>, shows that it was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>. Under the Chaulukya dynasty Vadodara gained importance: a plate dated 1077 mentions a battle on the bank of the Vishvamitri, and by then it was called a pura, or city, and the main centre of Jainism in the area had moved there.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> the city was home to Afghan amirs known as the Amir-i Sadgan, who revolted in 1344 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>; he defeated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a> in 1345. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a>, founder of the Gujarat Sultanate, made his son Firuz Khan governor of Vadodara, and Firuz claimed the throne after 1411 but gave up his claim on being forgiven. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1573 he gave Vadodara to Nawab Aurang Khan. In the late 1600s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> and Koli raids increased, and Kolis looted the city for two days in 1705.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 1,670,806 people in the Vadodara Municipal Corporation, which was founded in 1951, and about 3.5 million in the urban agglomeration. Gujarati is the first language of 71.37 per cent of residents, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the main religion. Vadodara is known as the Cultural City and the City of Art. It has landmarks such as the Laxmi Vilas Palace, the residence of the Maratha Gaekwad dynasty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja-sayajirao-university-of-baroda">Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda</a>.</p><p>Industry grew after oil and natural gas were found in the area, and the Gujarat Refinery, begun in 1963, was the third public-sector refinery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The city lies on the main rail and road routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vadodara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Damaji Rao II took Baroda (<b>Vadodara</b>) and much of the province, and the Gaekwads became one of the great Maratha houses alongside the Peshwas, Holkars and Scindias.</blockquote><a href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a></li><li><blockquote>Bhadarva was merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of Gujarat in 1960, in present <b>Vadodara</b> district.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhadarva-state">Bhadarva State</a></li></ul></section>
